# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────── the only requirement
# Any Postgres will do. Locally that can be one on your own machine:
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/kodukeel"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/kodukeel"
#
# Hosted (Vercel + Supabase): create a project at supabase.com, then use the
# dashboard's Connect panel. Both URLs come from the pooler.supabase.com host.
# Never use the direct db.<project-ref>.supabase.co host the dashboard offers
# first — it is IPv6-only and Vercel cannot reach it (P1001 on every build).
# DATABASE_URL is the transaction pooler (6543); ?pgbouncer=true is required
# or Prisma's prepared statements break against pgbouncer.
# DIRECT_URL is the session pooler (5432) — a full Postgres session, which is
# what lets `prisma db push` / `prisma migrate` run schema changes.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.xxxx:PASSWORD@aws-1-region.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.xxxx:PASSWORD@aws-1-region.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# ─── Sign-in (Supabase Auth + Google) — OPTIONAL ───────────────────────────
# Leave both blank to run in local mode: one learner, no accounts, everything in
# the database above. Fill them in and the app becomes multi-user with Google
# sign-in and every route gated. See docs/03-architecture.md ADR-013.
# Same Supabase project as above. Project Settings → API for these two — the
# anon/publishable key is designed to be public, it only ever authenticates
# who is signed in; it is never used to read or write app data directly.
# Google OAuth itself is configured in the Supabase dashboard, not here —
# see README.md "Deploying it as a real website".
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://xxxx.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# ─── Anu, the AI tutor (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Get a key at https://openrouter.ai → Keys → Create Key
# Without it, every other part of the app still works; only the tutor is off.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=""

# A SECOND PROVIDER IS AN AVAILABILITY FIX, NOT AN UPGRADE, AND IT IS FREE.
#
# The chain tries providers in order, free first, and walks past one that is
# throttled, having a bad minute, or out of credit. What it cannot walk past is
# the end of the chain. OpenRouter contributes one link per free model, so a
# chain four long can still be one account with one balance: when that balance
# ran out on the live deployment, all four answered 402 in the same second and
# Anu went down until it was topped up.
#
# Either of the two below is enough to prevent that, and neither asks for a
# card. They are tried before any paid key and only after the OpenRouter models
# have failed, so on an ordinary day they cost nothing and are never even
# reached.
#
#   Groq    https://console.groq.com → API Keys. Free tier, no card.
#   Gemini  https://aistudio.google.com/apikey → Create API key. Free tier, no card.
#
# WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU PICK ONE: a free tier is usually free because the
# provider reserves the right to look at what goes through it. Google says
# plainly that free-tier Gemini content may be used to improve their models;
# check Groq's current terms too. Everything a learner types to Anu goes to
# whichever provider answers, which is what /privacy already tells them, so
# choose the one whose terms you are willing to stand behind, and put the paid
# keys below in instead if neither is.
GROQ_API_KEY=""
GEMINI_API_KEY=""

# Optional, and only if you have credit. These sit at the end of the chain and
# are reached only when everything free has failed.
#
#   Anthropic  https://console.anthropic.com → API keys
#   OpenAI     https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
OPENAI_API_KEY=""

# Optional. Each defaults to a current model on that provider's free tier, and
# each accepts a comma-separated list tried in order. Leave them unset unless a
# name below has been retired, in which case the provider's own model list is
# the place to look.
# GROQ_MODEL="openai/gpt-oss-120b,qwen/qwen3.8-27b"
# GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-flash-latest,gemini-3.6-flash"
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL="claude-sonnet-5"
# OPENAI_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"
# LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you have credit and a reason. Anu already asks a
# chain of free models in order (lib/tutor/provider.ts), and setting this
# replaces that chain with whatever you name, so pinning one model is opting
# out of the fallback. A free model is rate-limited hard upstream, which is
# what the chain is for. Takes a comma-separated list if you want your own.
#
# Measured on this project's Estonian grammar questions (scripts/eval-anu.mjs),
# 2026-08-29:
#   google/gemma-4-31b-it:free              6/6   the default, and free
#   openai/gpt-4o                           6/6   roughly half a cent a question
#   minimax/minimax-m3:free                 5/6   second in the chain
#   nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free  5/6   third in the chain
#   anthropic/claude-sonnet-5               5/6
#   openai/gpt-4o-mini                      5/6   invented a nonsense Estonian example
#
# This note used to say that `*:free` models were too rate-limited to rely on,
# and that is why the default here was a paid model, and that is why a fresh
# key with no credit on it made Anu answer nothing. It was an artefact of the
# eval: it never read the HTTP status, so a 429 was an empty answer and scored
# as six wrong ones. Read properly, the free model at the top of this list
# answers every question the paid one does.
#OPENROUTER_MODEL=""

# Scan a page needs a model that can look at a photograph, and the free chain
# above is text-only. Scanning uses whatever is configured above unless this is
# set, deliberately: turning the camera on must never move a free deployment
# onto a paid model on its own. So scanning is off until you choose, and this
# is where you choose. A page costs roughly a third of a cent on gpt-4o.
#   OPENROUTER_VISION_MODEL="openai/gpt-4o"
# It applies to scanning and to nothing else. The same escape hatch exists for
# the other two providers, as ANTHROPIC_VISION_MODEL and OPENAI_VISION_MODEL.
#OPENROUTER_VISION_MODEL=""

# ─── Ekilex dictionary (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Free key from https://ekilex.ee → register → profile → API.
# Without it the dictionary searches the ~200 built-in words only.
EKILEX_API_KEY=""

# ─── Who runs this deployment ──────────────────────────────── set these three
# Not optional if anybody but you is going to use it. Kodukeel is software you
# install rather than a service with one address, so the person or school
# running this copy is the one legally answerable for the data in it: the
# controller under the GDPR and the service provider under the Estonian
# Information Society Services Act. Both want a name, a place and a direct
# electronic contact shown to the reader, and /privacy and /terms render
# exactly what is set here.
#
# Leave them blank and both pages say, in as many words, that the operator has
# not been named. That is deliberate. A page that quietly says nothing looks
# finished; a page that says it is missing gets fixed.
#
# The registry code is Estonia's eight-digit business register number and is
# only for a company. A parent running this for one family is still a
# controller and simply has no code, which is why it is the one that is
# genuinely optional.
OPERATOR_NAME=""
OPERATOR_ADDRESS=""
OPERATOR_EMAIL=""
OPERATOR_REGISTRY_CODE=""

# ─── Who may sign in (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave both blank and sign-up is open to anyone with a Google account — the
# per-user AI quota below, not a guest list, is what keeps an open door from
# becoming an open bill. Set either one to turn this deployment into a private
# instance for a class or a family. Comma-separated; matched on the whole
# domain, so "kool.ee" does not admit "evilkool.ee".
ALLOWED_EMAILS=""
ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=""

# ─── Who reviews suggested fixes (set this if anyone else uses it) ─────────
# Every dead end in the app offers to send a suggested fix: a missing word, a
# gloss that is the wrong sense, a form that is wrong, an answer marked wrong
# that was right, a screen that failed. They land in a review queue at
# /admin/suggestions, and accepting a dictionary correction there writes it
# into the entry every learner reads.
#
# So this is the shortest list in this file, and it is exact addresses only,
# comma-separated. No domain rule: "this school may sign in" and "this person
# may change what everybody reads" are different questions.
#
# Leave it blank on a deployment with sign-in and nobody reviews anything: the
# queue tells whoever opens it that no reviewer has been named, rather than
# showing an empty list that looks like an empty queue. Reports are still
# collected and nothing is lost. In local mode, with no sign-in configured,
# there is one learner on one machine and they review their own.
ADMIN_EMAILS=""

# ─── AI spend limits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app is free to use and these are what keep it that way. Every one has a
# default and there is no way to turn metering off. The tutor is the only
# metered path: review, the dictionary, every drill and the deck are never
# limited. Set AI_DAILY_USD_GLOBAL="0" to stop AI spending entirely.
#
# AI_DAILY_CALLS_PER_USER is the tutor's allowance. The other two paths scale
# off it in lib/usage/ledger.ts, because a grader note and a cache miss on
# speech do not cost what a tutoring conversation costs: the writing grader
# gets three times this, and speech thirty times, since it is free and cached.
AI_BURST_CALLS="8"
AI_BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS="60"
AI_DAILY_CALLS_PER_USER="10"
AI_DAILY_USD_PER_USER="0.50"
AI_DAILY_USD_GLOBAL="20"

# The tail of the day's shared budget held back for people who have not asked
# anything yet, so a busy morning cannot leave a newcomer with a dead tutor.
# Once spending reaches it, anyone who has already had this many answers waits
# until midnight UTC and anyone who has not can still ask.
AI_GLOBAL_RESERVE_FRACTION="0.25"
AI_RESERVE_CALLS_PER_USER="3"

# ─── Aggregate metrics (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and GET /api/metrics returns cohort retention, weekly actives and
# dictionary size, computed from the append-only review log. Nothing extra is
# collected about anybody to produce it, and nothing that leaves the endpoint
# identifies a person: cohorts smaller than five report their size but not
# their rates. Leave it unset and the route 404s.
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $METRICS_TOKEN" https://your-app/api/metrics
METRICS_TOKEN=""

# ─── Shared audio cache (recommended when hosted) ──────────────────────────
# Without this, synthesised speech is cached on local disk — which on a
# serverless host is per-instance and wiped on every cold start, so every cold
# start re-asks TartuNLP for words it has already spoken. With it, one clip is
# fetched once for everyone, forever.
# It has a second job, and it is the one that matters legally: erasing an
# account means erasing the sign-in record too, and that lives in Supabase Auth
# rather than in this app's tables. Without this key the delete button removes
# every row and then tells the learner plainly that their email address is
# still held by the sign-in provider and who to ask. With it, that is gone too.
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role key. SERVER ONLY:
# this key bypasses row-level security. CI fails the build if it reaches the
# browser bundle.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""
SUPABASE_AUDIO_BUCKET="audio"

# ─── Trusting a proxy's forwarded-for header (self-hosted only) ────────────
# On Vercel this is handled for you: the platform sets x-vercel-forwarded-for
# and overwrites it on every request, so it can be believed and this stays
# unset. Anywhere else, X-Forwarded-For is just a header, and a caller sitting
# directly in front of Node can write whatever they like in it.
#
# So it is not read unless you say it can be. That is not caution for its own
# sake: the rate limiter buckets an anonymous caller by address, and a caller
# who picks their own address gets a fresh allowance on every request — an
# unlimited number of limits, which is not a limit. Untrusted, every
# unattributed request shares one bucket instead, which is the honest shape
# for a deployment that genuinely cannot tell two strangers apart.
#
# Set this to "1" only if a reverse proxy in front of this app strips or
# overwrites incoming X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP headers. Signed-in
# requests are charged to the learner and never touch any of this.
TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=""

# ─── Error reporting (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and every handled error is POSTed to it as JSON: where it happened,
# the message, and the opaque user id — never an email address, and never a
# value that looks like a credential, which lib/observability/report.ts strips
# before anything is sent.
#
# It is still a third party receiving personal data, so setting it puts one
# more recipient on /privacy, automatically, named as an error-reporting
# endpoint. That is the whole reason it is documented here rather than left as
# a variable somebody discovers in the source: an operator turning this on is
# making a disclosure decision, and the page makes it for them.
ERROR_WEBHOOK_URL=""
